[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Configure and operate the DMS (/docs/pr-preview/pr-78/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms)\n\n\n\nA **Device Management Service (DMS)** configures admission and identity lifecycle for its devices. It acts as a registration authority; the CA selected in `Enrollment CA` signs certificates. This guide is for the policy administrator.\n\nBefore you begin [#before-you-begin]\n\nYou need DMS management permissions, an active CA with a usable key and a tested [issuance profile](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). Agree device ID, initial credential, consumer and renewal window with the integrator. Review [Provisioning strategies](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies) before choosing registration and replacement.\n\nPrepare EST server trust and device connectivity through [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking). Your operator’s administrative authentication does not replace device EST authentication.\n\nConfigure a DMS [#configure-a-dms]\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step>\n    Identify the fleet [#identify-the-fleet]\n\n    Create the DMS with a recognizable name and retain its ID. EST URLs use this identifier, not the display name. Each device retains one owning DMS.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Select the enrollment CA [#select-the-enrollment-ca]\n\n    Choose **Enrollment CA** and an explicit profile appropriate for the end-entity identity. Keep CN compatible with `Device ID`. The test certificate should allow its intended purpose and expire within issuer validity.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Configure EST [#configure-est]\n\n    Select initial enrollment registration and authentication. Separately configure re-enrollment authentication, thresholds and previous certificate retirement. Leave ServerKeyGen disabled if the client generates its key.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Distribute trust [#distribute-trust]\n\n    Select certificates returned by `/cacerts`. Explicitly include required intermediates and roots, keeping distribution distinct from automatic trust installation.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Verify the endpoints [#verify-the-endpoints]\n\n    Save and read configuration again. Query `/cacerts`, perform an allowed and a rejected enrollment, and inspect device, serial and history. Creating the DMS or downloading cacerts does not establish working authentication and issuance.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\nProtocol configuration [#protocol-configuration]\n\nSee [Configure EST in the DMS](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/configuration) for registration, authentication, issuance, renewal, key generation and trust distribution.\n\nOperate the DMS inventory [#operate-the-dms-inventory]\n\n**Edit** changes name, settings and metadata. Updates go through the [DMS API](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager). `PUT /v1/dms/{id}` always targets the DMS identified by the URL path: the body carries no `id` field, and any `id` or `creation_ts` in it is silently ignored. Authorization is evaluated against the same URL identifier. The body must include a non-empty `name` and a `settings` object; missing either returns HTTP 400 and leaves the stored DMS unchanged. `name` and `settings` are always full replacements, so read the DMS before sending API edits to avoid losing fields. `metadata` is optional: omitting it or sending `null` keeps the stored metadata, a supplied object replaces it entirely, and `{}` clears it (a later GET returns an empty object, not `null`). Old-style bodies that carry the full DMS object keep working while they include `name` and `settings`; unknown fields are ignored. Updates reject an empty `name` with HTTP 400: a DMS whose stored name was zeroed by an earlier update must be renamed before any client round-trips it, including the AWS IoT connector’s JITP writeback. If the object does not include the AWS IoT connector key (`lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`), the connector ignores the DMS's later events.\n\n**Go to DMS owned devices** restricts inventory by owner. **Show/Edit Metadata** contains operational and integration data; consult the integration guide before changing it. **EST (RFC-7030)** helps locate URLs.\n\n**Delete** removes the DMS record and configuration; it does not itself cascade revocation across every device and certificate. Migrate dependencies and retain audit references before retirement.\n\nIntegrate the devices [#integrate-the-devices]\n\nContinue with [EST client](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/configuration) or the [enrollment and mTLS tutorial](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device). [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/configuration) covers formats; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/troubleshooting) covers failures.\n"]
